-- The store conformance capture. The ONE tool in this package that needs a database, which is why
-- it is an executable and not a test: under the three-tier decision nothing in `cabal test` opens
-- a socket, and the artifact this writes is what the suite asserts over, offline.
--
-- `aeson` is here deliberately. It writes the JSON REPORT -- a description of an experiment -- and
-- never touches an artifact byte; BYTE-03's source scan is scoped to the library's storage modules
-- for exactly that reason. `crypton` computes the migration digests the freshness oracle recomputes
-- (already a library dependency, so no new package enters the build plan), and `postgresql-simple`
-- appears because the corpus's BROKEN path needs one deliberately un-wrapped parameter that must
-- not be shipped in the library.
executable store-conformance
    import:           warnings
    main-is:          StoreConformance.hs
    build-depends:
        base ^>=4.20.2.0,
        cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api,
        aeson,
        bytestring,
        text,
        process,
        directory,
        filepath,
        crypton,
        postgresql-simple,
        postgresql-migration
    hs-source-dirs:   offchain/app
    default-language: Haskell2010

-- The GAMS conformance capture. The ONE tool in this package that needs a solver, which is why it
-- is an executable and not a test: under the three-tier decision nothing in `cabal test` invokes
-- GAMS, and the artifact this writes is what the suite asserts over, offline. It was the ONLY
-- importer of Gams.Invoke until the end-to-end spike below; the property that matters is not the
-- COUNT but the DIRECTORY -- every importer is under offchain/app/ and none is under offchain/test/
-- -- and that is what makes "the suite is structurally incapable of shelling out to the solver" a
-- fact about the import graph rather than a convention. The enforcing check is the suite's own
-- the_suite_never_names_the_real_solver, whose subject is offchain/test/Main.hs alone; there is no
-- importer-count check anywhere, and this comment is not one.
--
-- `aeson` is here deliberately, and for exactly the reason it is in the store-conformance stanza
-- above. It writes the JSON REPORT -- a description of an experiment -- and never touches an
-- artifact byte: every byte-level fact in that report travels as a length and a BARE hex digest
-- computed by Store.Types.sha256_hex over the bytes themselves. BYTE-03's source scan is scoped to
-- the library's storage modules for precisely that reason, so a report that never carries bytes
-- cannot re-render a number or reorder a key. NO NEW PACKAGE: aeson, bytestring, directory,
-- filepath and process are all already library dependencies.
executable gams-conformance
    import:           warnings
    main-is:          GamsConformance.hs
    build-depends:
        base ^>=4.20.2.0,
        cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api,
        aeson,
        bytestring,
        directory,
        filepath,
        process
    hs-source-dirs:   offchain/app
    default-language: Haskell2010

-- The FEE-SPLIT differential capture. The second tool here that needs a solver, and the only one
-- whose subject is the prover REFUSING something: it drives twelve grid points one pip either side
-- of four exact admissibility boundaries and records the model's own abort LINE NUMBER, which is
-- the discriminator the exit code cannot supply (exit 3 means at least six different things).
--
-- It is an executable and not a test for the same reason gams-conformance is: under the three-tier
-- decision nothing in `cabal test` invokes GAMS, and the artifact this writes is what the suite
-- asserts over, offline. It is the THIRD importer of Gams.Invoke and, like the other two, it lives
-- under offchain/app/ -- which is the structural property, not the count.
--
-- It is also the ONE permitted consumer of Gams.Argv.render_argv_ungated. That is asserted by the
-- suite's own the_ungated_renderer_has_exactly_one_consumer, in BOTH directions over a scan of
-- offchain/, because a second consumer is how "an inadmissible shock is refused before any
-- subprocess" quietly stops being true.
--
-- `aeson` is here on the same ruling as the two capture stanzas above: it writes the JSON REPORT
-- and no artifact byte reaches it -- every byte-level fact travels as a BARE hex digest computed by
-- Store.Types.sha256_hex. NO NEW PACKAGE: aeson, bytestring, directory, filepath and process are
-- all already library dependencies.
executable fee-split-conformance
    import:           warnings
    main-is:          FeeSplitConformance.hs
    build-depends:
        base ^>=4.20.2.0,
        cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api,
        aeson,
        bytestring,
        directory,
        filepath,
        process
    hs-source-dirs:   offchain/app
    default-language: Haskell2010

-- THE END-TO-END SPIKE. THROWAWAY BY CONSTRUCTION -- not a phase, not a requirement, not a check,
-- and nothing depends on it. It exists because, measured after phases 23-26 shipped 8,640 lines of
-- library code and 181 checks, NO app called Store.Cache.decide and NOTHING wired Gams.Run into the
-- store's Solver seam: every component was proven at its seam against stubs and they had never met.
-- Phase 28 was the first place they would, and it is the phase with the least slack, so this moves
-- that meeting forward to where failure is cheap. Delete it whenever it stops being useful.
--
-- It is an executable and NOT a test for the same reason gams-conformance is: it needs a solver,
-- and under the three-tier decision nothing in `cabal test` invokes GAMS. It is the SECOND importer
-- of Gams.Invoke, and it imports it for the RESOLVERS only -- resolve_gams_bin, resolve_gams_model
-- and the two budget constants -- because writing a second resolver would be a second place for the
-- same value to differ, which is the defect that module's own haddock exists to prevent. The
-- structural property is unchanged: every importer of that module is under offchain/app/, and
-- offchain/test/Main.hs still names it nowhere.
--
-- NO NEW PACKAGE, and no aeson: this tool writes no report. bytestring reads and writes the
-- artifact's bytes, directory supplies the scratch directory and the home directory the whitelist
-- is built from, and filepath supplies the model's basename. All three are already library
-- dependencies, so nothing new enters the build plan.
executable spike-end-to-end
    import:           warnings
    main-is:          SpikeEndToEnd.hs
    build-depends:
        base ^>=4.20.2.0,
        cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api,
        bytestring,
        directory,
        filepath
    hs-source-dirs:   offchain/app
    default-language: Haskell2010

test-suite cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api-test
    -- Import common warning flags.
    import:           warnings

    -- Base language which the package is written in.
    default-language: Haskell2010

    -- Modules included in this executable, other than Main.
    -- other-modules:

    -- LANGUAGE extensions used by modules in this package.
    -- other-extensions:

    -- The interface type and version of the test suite.
    type:             exitcode-stdio-1.0

    -- Directories containing source files.
    hs-source-dirs:   offchain/test

    -- The entrypoint to the test suite.
    main-is:          Main.hs

    -- Test dependencies.
    --
    -- The last four exist so the suite can exercise the LIBRARY's own wire types rather than a
    -- transcription of them: memory-hexstring for the HexString the encoder returns,
    -- web3-ethereum for Quantity and Change (the Phase 21 event re-pin builds synthetic logs),
    -- web3-solidity for the Address that Change's non-optional changeAddress field demands, and
    -- mwc-random for the fixed-seed GenIO the drawn-order checks need. All four are already
    -- project dependencies of the library, so no new package enters the build plan.
    build-depends:
        base ^>=4.20.2.0,
        cfmm-replicationPlank-rpc-api,
        web3-crypto,
        aeson,
        bytestring,
        text,
        containers,
        process,
        directory,
        filepath,
        memory-hexstring,
        web3-ethereum,
        web3-solidity,
        mwc-random,
        -- 23-05: the conformance freshness oracle RECOMPUTES each migration's md5 from
        -- offchain/migrations/ and compares it to the digest the capture recorded, so a .sql edited
        -- without a re-capture reddens. postgresql-migration stores md5, so md5 is the algorithm
        -- the comparison has to speak. NOT a new package: crypton-1.0.6 is already resolved in
        -- this build plan through the library stanza (and through web3-crypto's crypton <1.1 pin),
        -- so `cabal build --enable-tests -j all` prints no Downloading for it.
        crypton
